url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/1580175\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'JSTOR', 'container_title': 'Oriens', 'volume': '23', 'issue': '', 'page': '614', 'issn': ['0078-6527'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '1121', 'prefix': '10.2307'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/412267\",\n  \"title\": \"<b>Predvariteťnoe soobščenie ob issledovanii proto-indijskix tekstov.</b>\\n                    [Preliminary report on the investigation of the Proto-Indian script.] By Yu. V. Knorozov et al. Moskva: Institut Etnografii AN SSSR, 1965. Pp. 74. -\\n                    <b>Proto-Indica: 1968.</b>\\n                    Brief report on the investigation of the Proto-Indian texts. By Yu. V. Knorozov et al. Moscow: Academy of Sciences of the USSR, Institute of Ethnography, 1968. Pp. 55. -\\n                    <b>Decipherment of the Proto-Dravidian inscriptions of the Indus Civilization.</b>\\n                    By Asko Parpola, Seppo Koskenniemi, Simo Parpola, and Pentti Aalto. (Scandinavian Institute of Asian Studies, special publications, 1.) Copenhagen, 1969. Pp. 72. -\\n                    <b>Progress in the decipherment of the Proto-Dravidian Indus script.</b>\\n                    By Asko Parpola, Seppo Koskenniemi, Simo Parpola, and Pentti Aalto. (Scandinavian Institute of Asian Studies, special publications, 2.) Copenhagen, 1969. Pp. 47. -\\n                    <b>Further progress in the Indus script decipherment.</b>\\n                    By Asko Parpola, Seppo Koskenniemi, Simo Parpola, and Pentti Aalto. (Scandinavian Institute of Asian Studies, special publications, 3.) Copenhagen, 1970. Pp. 46.\",\n  \"authors\": \"Arlene R. K. Zide; Kamil V. Zvelebil\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2307/412267\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1970-12-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/412267\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 3,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Language', 'volume': '46', 'issue': '4', 'page': '952-968', 'issn': ['0097-8507', '1535-0665'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1515/kadm.1971.10.1.10\",\n  \"title\": \"COMPUTER TECHNIQUES IN THE STUDY OF THE INDUS SCRIPT\",\n  \"authors\": \"ASKO PARPOLA\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1515/kadm.1971.10.1.10\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1971-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1515/kadm.1971.10.1.10\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 1,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Walter de Gruyter GmbH', 'container_title': 'Kadmos', 'volume': '10', 'issue': '1', 'page': '', 'issn': ['0022-7498', '1613-0723'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '374', 'prefix': '10.1515'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.31235/osf.io/ftdzc\",\n  \"title\": \"Can the semasiographic/logographic Indus script answer the Dravidian question? Insights from Indus script’s gemstone related fish-signs, and Indus gemstone-word “maṇi”\",\n  \"authors\": \"Bahata Ansumali Mukhopadhyay\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>From archaeogenetic, linguistic, archaeological, and ethnographic evidences, several scholars have linked the people of ancient Indus valley civilization (IVC) with the ancestors of Dravidian speakers. This article argues that the semasiographically/logographically written Indus script (ISC) used certain fish-like logograms, whose iconicity emerged from certain Indus symbolisms, which are linkable with certain linguistic symbolisms found across today’s Dravidian languages. As discussed in the author’s previous articles, ISC was a mercantile script, and different segments of the formulaic ISC inscriptions were usually populated by signs belonging to specific semantic classes.  The core-informational segment, which occur preceding the phrase-final and pre-phrase-final signs ( ), arguably encoded names of licensed crafts, and taxed commodities (e.g., the crucible-blowpipe based sign   which signified precious-metals/gold-smithy). This article claims that the fish-like ISC signs ( , , , , , ,  ), which mainly occur in the ‘core-informational’ segments, often accompanying the gold/precious-metal sign   (e.g.,  ,  ), possibly encoded meanings related to gemstones, lapidary activities, related precious commodities, barter equivalencies, and taxes/licenses. Interestingly, in various Indus settlements, seals/tablets containing fish-sign- inscriptions are found in unusually high concentration in the archaeological contexts of lapidary workshops and jeweler’s shops. Revealingly, the polysemic Proto-Dravidian fish-word “mīn”, a popular fish-word used across present India, also signifies ‘shining’, ‘bright’, and ‘gemstone’ in several Dravidian languages, and is etymologically connected to the Proto-Dravidian root-verb “*mīn”, which signifies “to shine,” “to glitter,” etc. In the Indian subcontinent, this fish-based homonymy for gemstones and related linguistic symbolism for other glittering things (“*min”-based words coined for stars, fireflies, lightning, etc.) are found only in the Dravidian language-group, indicating that the gemstone related fish-sign symbolism of ISC might have emerged from an ancestral Dravidian culture prevalent in IVC.  Relevantly, in Mesopotamia, the eye-patterned apotropaic gemstone-beads imported from IVC were called “fish-eye” stones (“NA4-IGI- ḪA”, “NA4-IGI-KU6”), and \\\"eye-stones\\\" of Meluhha (\\\"NA4-IGI-ME-LUḪ-ḪA\\\"), whereas in Dravidian languages, carnelian (“akki-kal”), tiger-eye-like sardonyx (“puli-k-kaṇ-kal”), and cat’s-eye-like chrysoberyl (“pūṉai-k-kaṇ-kal”) have eye-stone related epithets. Moreover, “maṇi”, the Indus word for gemstones, which remained fossilized in ancient documents of Near East, arguably has a Dravidian root. Intriguingly, “maṇi” has been signifying meanings like “amulet” (see Atharvaveda), “bead”, “apple of eye”, and “gemstone” in Indian languages, each which were linked to the amuletic, eye-patterned gemstone beads of Meluhah.</p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.31235/osf.io/ftdzc\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2023-04-08T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/ftdzc\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Center for Open Science', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '15934', 'prefix': '10.31235'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1017/s0035869x00128461\",\n  \"title\": \"Decipherment of the proto-Dravidian inscriptions of the Indus civilization. A First Announcement. Progress in the decipherment of the proto-Dravidian Indus script. By Asko Parpola and others. (The Scandinavian Institute of Asian Studies Special Publications Nos. 1 and 2.) pp. 72 and 47. Copenhagen, 1969.\",\n  \"authors\": \"William C. Brice\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1017/s0035869x00128461\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1970-04-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1017/s0035869x00128461\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 1,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society of Great Britain &amp; Ireland', 'volume': '102', 'issue': '2', 'page': '221-223', 'issn': ['0035-869X', '2051-2066'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2139/ssrn.4412558\",\n  \"title\": \"Can the Semasiographic/logographic Indus Script Answer the Dravidian Question? Insights from Indus Script's Gemstone Related Fish-Signs, and Indus Gemstone-Word 'maṇi'\",\n  \"authors\": \"Bahata A. Mukhopadhyay\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>From archaeogenetic, linguistic, archaeological, and ethnographic evidences, several scholars have linked the people of ancient Indus valley civilization (IVC) with the ancestors of Dravidian speakers. This article argues that the semasiographically/logographically written Indus script (ISC) used certain fish-like logograms, whose iconicity emerged from certain Indus symbolisms, which are linkable with certain linguistic symbolisms found across today’s Dravidian languages. As discussed in the author’s previous articles, ISC was a mercantile script, and different segments of the formulaic ISC inscriptions were usually populated by signs belonging to specific semantic classes. The core-informational segment, which occur preceding the phrase-final and pre-phrase-final signs ( ), arguably encoded names of licensed crafts, and taxed commodities (e.g., the crucible-blowpipe based sign which signified precious-metals/gold-smithy). This article claims that the fish-like ISC signs which mainly occur in the ‘core-informational’ segments, often accompanying the gold/precious-metal sign possibly encoded meanings related to gemstones, lapidary activities, related precious commodities, barter equivalencies, and taxes/licenses. Interestingly, in various Indus settlements, seals/tablets containing fish-sign- inscriptions are found in unusually high concentration in the archaeological contexts of lapidary workshops and jeweler’s shops. Revealingly, the polysemic Proto-Dravidian fish-word “mīn”, a popular fish-word used across present India, also signifies ‘shining’, ‘bright’, and ‘gemstone’ in several Dravidian languages, and is etymologically connected to the Proto-Dravidian root-verb “*mīn”, which signifies “to shine,” “to glitter,” etc. In the Indian subcontinent, this fish-based homonymy for gemstones and related linguistic symbolism for other glittering things (“*min”-based words coined for stars, fireflies, lightning, etc.) are found only in the Dravidian language-group, indicating that the gemstone related fish-sign symbolism of ISC might have emerged from an ancestral Dravidian culture prevalent in IVC. Relevantly, in Mesopotamia, the eye-patterned apotropaic gemstone-beads imported from IVC were called “fish-eye” stones (“NA4-IGI- ḪA”, “NA4-IGI-KU6”), and \\\"eye-stones\\\" of Meluhha (\\\"NA4-IGI-ME-LUḪ-ḪA\\\"), whereas in Dravidian languages, carnelian (“akki-kal”), tiger-eye-like sardonyx (“puli-k-kaṇ-kal”), and cat’s-eye-like chrysoberyl (“pūṉai-k-kaṇ-kal”) have eye-stone related epithets. Moreover, “maṇi”, the Indus word for gemstones, which remained fossilized in ancient documents of Near East, arguably has a Dravidian root. Intriguingly, “maṇi” has been signifying meanings like “amulet” (see Atharvaveda), “bead”, “apple of eye”, and “gemstone” in Indian languages, each which were linked to the amuletic, eye-patterned gemstone beads of Meluhah.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2139/ssrn.4412558\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2023-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4412558\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.2139'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/599265\",\n  \"title\": \"Materials for the Study of the Indus Script. I: A Concordance to the Indus Inscriptions\",\n  \"authors\": \"M. B. Emeneau; Seppo Koskenniemi; Asko Parpola; Simo Parpola\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2307/599265\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1975-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/599265\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'JSTOR', 'container_title': 'Journal of the American Oriental Society', 'volume': '95', 'issue': '1', 'page': '138', 'issn': ['0003-0279'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '1121', 'prefix': '10.2307'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1080/00438243.1986.9979979\",\n  \"title\": \"The Indus script: A challenging puzzle\",\n  \"authors\": \"Asko Parpola\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1080/00438243.1986.9979979\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1986-02-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1080/00438243.1986.9979979\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 17,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Informa UK Limited', 'container_title': 'World Archaeology', 'volume': '17', 'issue': '3', 'page': '399-419', 'issn': ['0043-8243', '1470-1375'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '301', 'prefix': '10.1080'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1525/aa.1996.98.1.02a00470\",\n  \"title\": \"<i>Deciphering the Indus Script.</i> Asko Parpola\",\n  \"authors\": \"Jonathan Mark Kenoyer\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1525/aa.1996.98.1.02a00470\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1996-03-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1525/aa.1996.98.1.02a00470\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Wiley', 'container_title': 'American Anthropologist', 'volume': '98', 'issue': '1', 'page': '198-199', 'issn': ['0002-7294', '1548-1433'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '311', 'prefix': '10.1111'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/605446\",\n  \"title\": \"Deciphering the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Richard Salomon; Asko Parpola\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2307/605446\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1996-10-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/605446\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 2,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'JSTOR', 'container_title': 'Journal of the American Oriental Society', 'volume': '116', 'issue': '4', 'page': '745', 'issn': ['0003-0279'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '1121', 'prefix': '10.2307'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1017/s0003598x00040709\",\n  \"title\": \"Dravidian and the Decipherment of the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"T. Burrow\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>The Indus script has been known for a little over 40 years, and it has so far resisted all attempts at decipherment. This is not surprising in view of the fact that the inscriptions are all very short, and that no long connected texts are preserved. It is also not known what language lies behind the writing: bilinguals are nonexistent, and given the historical circumstances, not likely to be found. In the period since the discovery of the inscriptions a number of attempts at decipherment has been made, but none has carried conviction. Quite recently there has been a number of fresh attempts, of which the most sustained and ambitious, by a group of Finnish scholars, has been described in the previous issue of this journal. It is based on the assumption that the language spoken in the Indus cities was a form of Proto-Dravidian. The detailed methods used in this attempt at decipherment have been briefly described in the previous articles, and they need not be repeated here. Instead it is proposed to examine the handling of the Dravidian material, which, as will be seen, is open to considerable criticism</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1017/s0003598x00040709\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1969-12-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1017/s0003598x00040709\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 5,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Antiquity', 'volume': '43', 'issue': '172', 'page': '274-278', 'issn': ['0003-598X', '1745-1744'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1163/9789004668645_007\",\n  \"title\": \"Religion Reflected in the Iconic Signs of the Indus Script: Penetrating into Long-Forgotten Picto + Graphic Messages\",\n  \"authors\": \"Asko Parpola\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1163/9789004668645_007\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1988-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1163/9789004668645_007\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'BRILL', 'container_title': 'The Image in Writing', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '114-135', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9789004668645', '9789004085473'], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '374', 'prefix': '10.1163'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/416809\",\n  \"title\": \"<b>Deciphering the Indus script.</b>\\n                    By Asko Parpola. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1994. Pp. 374. £